{
  "module": "Input side: text to vectors",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "embeddings",
    "title": "Embeddings and the embedding table",
    "summary": "How a token ID becomes a vector: what an embedding and d_model are, the table E and the lookup, and the matrix X₀ the transformer starts from.",
    "covers": ["embedding", "embedding-table"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "tokens",
        "reason": "An embedding is what a token ID is turned into."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "sequence-shapes",
        "reason": "The table E is described by its shape, and n × d_model must already be readable."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "labels",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A token ID is just a position in the vocabulary list. Token 4217 is not 'a bit more' than token 4216 in any meaningful way: the numbers are "
            },
            {
              "text": "labels",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", like locker numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "If the network were handed the ID as a plain number, it would have almost nothing useful to work with: the number does not say which tokens are related. We need a richer description of each token."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 3, 6)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "vector",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "That richer description is an "
            },
            {
              "text": "embedding",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": a learned list of real numbers (a "
            },
            {
              "text": "vector",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ") that represents a token as a point in a high-dimensional space."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It is "
            },
            {
              "text": "learned",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": the numbers are adjusted during training. The useful result is that tokens used in similar ways tend to end up with similar vectors."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 6)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "width",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Every token's vector has the same length. That length is called "
            },
            {
              "text": "d_model",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", the model's basic width."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "d_model stays constant through the whole network: every layer takes vectors of length d_model and returns vectors of length d_model."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "You met d_model as a column count in the last lesson. It is the number of columns in the table that holds the sequence."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 6)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "table-e",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Where do the vectors live? In one big table, the "
            },
            {
              "text": "embedding table",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", written "
            },
            {
              "text": "E",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". It has one row for every token in the vocabulary, and each row is that token's vector."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "So E has "
            },
            {
              "text": "V rows",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " (one per vocabulary entry) and "
            },
            {
              "text": "d_model columns",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": it is a "
            },
            {
              "text": "V × d_model",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " matrix."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 29)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "lookup",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Turning a token ID into a vector is a "
            },
            {
              "text": "lookup",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": token ID "
            },
            {
              "text": "i",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": " selects row "
            },
            {
              "text": "i",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": " of E, written "
            },
            {
              "text": "E[i]",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The same thing can be written as multiplication: a vector that is 1 in position "
            },
            {
              "text": "i",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": " and 0 everywhere else (a "
            },
            {
              "text": "one-hot",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": " vector) multiplied by E gives row "
            },
            {
              "text": "i",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". The lookup is simply the fast way to do that multiplication."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The network is never given the text or the IDs directly as numbers to compute with. It is given the rows E selects."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 29)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "x0",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A text of n tokens has n IDs. Look up each one and stack the n rows in order. The result is called "
            },
            {
              "text": "X₀",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " (say 'X zero'), an "
            },
            {
              "text": "n × d_model",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " matrix."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "X₀ is where the transformer's real work starts: one d_model-long vector per token, in order."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 29)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "size",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Because E holds V rows of d_model numbers, it has "
            },
            {
              "text": "V · d_model",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " parameters (learned numbers)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "With a small made-up example, V = 1,000 and d_model = 8 gives 1,000 × 8 = 8,000 numbers."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "A lookup costs almost nothing to compute next to the transformer blocks, but the table itself can be big when V is large."
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 29)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "What is an embedding?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "correct": true,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "A learned vector of real numbers that represents a token"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "It places the token as a point in a high-dimensional space, and similar tokens tend to get similar vectors."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The token's ID, written in a different base"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "An ID is only a label. An embedding is a learned vector with many numbers."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The text of the token, encoded as bytes"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The text is not what the network works with; the embedding is learned during training."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "A count of how often the token appears"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "An embedding is not a count; it is a learned vector."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "teaches": ["vector"],
      "covers": ["embedding"],
